s inducing a speed-up factor of O(d\nL185@P3: 2\nL186@P3: ), for d the extension degree. See e.g. [4,\nL187@P3: §4] for more details.\nL188@P3: 2.5 Atomic comparisons\nL189@P3: We first present a comparison between the preceding implementation possibilities. The idea is to com\u0002pare just the atomic operations. “%” denotes an implementation using machine remaindering (machine\nL190@P3: division) for every operation. This is just to give a comparing scale. “NTL” denotes NTL’s floating point\nL191@P3: flooring for multiplication ; “Z/pZ” denotes our implementation of the classical representation when tests\nL192@P3: ensure that machine remaindering is used only when really needed. Last “GFq” denotes the discrete\nL193@P3: logarithm implementation of section 2.4. In order to be able to compare those single operations, the\nL194@P3: experiment is an application of the arithmetic operator on vectors of a given size (e.g. 256 for figures 2,\nL195@P3: 3 and 4).\nL196@P3: \u0013\nL197@P3: \u0018\nL198@P3: \u0014\u0013\nL199@P3: \u0014\u0018\nL200@P3: \u0015\u0013\nL201@P3: \u0015\u0018\nL202@P3: \u0016\u0013\nL203@P3: \u0016\u0018\nL204@P3: \u0017\u0013\nL205@P3: $GGLWLRQ 6XEWUDFWLRQ 1HJDWLRQ 0XOWLSOLFDWLRQ 'LYLVLRQ $;3<,1 $;3<\nL206@P3: 6SHHG\u0003\nL207@P3: 0RS\u0012V\nL208@P3:  \b =\u0012S= 17/ *)T\nL209@P3: Fig. 2. Single arithmetic operation modulo 32749 on a sparc ultra II, 250 MHz\nL210@P3: We compare the number of millions of field arithmetic operations per second, Mop/s.\nL211@P3-4: Figure 2 shows the results on a UltraSparc II 250 Mhz. First one can see that the need of Euclid’s algo\u0002rithm for the field division is a huge drawback of all the implementations save one. Indeed division overFinite field dot product 5\nL212@P4: \u0013\nL213@P4: \u0015\u0013\nL214@P4: \u0017\u0013\nL215@P4: \u0019\u0013\nL216@P4: \u001b\u0013\nL217@P4: \u0014\u0013\u0013\nL218@P4: \u0014\u0015\u0013\nL219@P4: \u0014\u0017\u0013\nL220@P4: \u0014\u0019\u0013\nL221@P4: \u0014\u001b\u0013\nL222@P4: \u0015\u0013\u0013\nL223@P4: $GGLWLRQ 6XEWUDFWLRQ 1HJDWLRQ 0XOWLSOLFDWLRQ 'LYLVLRQ $;3<,1 $;3<\nL224@P4: 6SHHG\u0003\nL225@P4: 0RS\u0012V\nL226@P4:  0RQWJRPHU\\ =\u0012S= 17/ *)T\nL227@P4: Fig. 3. Single arithmetic operation modulo 32749 on a Pentium III, 1 GHz\nL228@P4: \u0013\nL229@P4: \u0018\u0013\nL230@P4: \u0014\u0013\u0013\nL231@P4: \u0014\u0018\u0013\nL232@P4: \u0015\u0013\u0013\nL233@P4: \u0015\u0018\u0013\nL234@P4: \u0016\u0013\u0013\nL235@P4: \u0016\u0018\u0013\nL236@P4: \u0017\u0013\u0013\nL237@P4: \u0017\u0018\u0013\nL238@P4: \u0018\u0013\u0013\nL239@P4: $GGLWLRQ 6XEWUDFWLRQ 1HJDWLRQ 0XOWLSOLFDWLRQ 'LYLVLRQ $;3<,1 $;3<\nL240@P4: 6SHHG\u0003\nL241@P4: 0RS\u0012V\nL242@P4:  0RQWJRPHU\\ =\u0012S= 17/ *)T\nL243@P4-5: Fig. 4. Single arithmetic operation modulo 32749 on a Pentium IV, 2.4 GHz6 Jean-Guillaume Dumas\nL244@P5: “GFq” are just an index subtraction. Next, we see that floating point operations are quite faster than\nL245@P5: integer operations: indeed, NTL’s multiplication is better than the integer one. Now, on this machine,\nL246@P5: memory accesses are not yet much slower than arithmetic operations. This is the reason why discrete\nL247@P5: logarithm addition is only 2 to 3 times slower than one arithmetic call. This, enables the “GFq” AXPY\nL248@P5: (base operation of most of the linear algebra operators) to be the fastest.\nL249@P5: On newer PC, namely Intel Pentium III and IV of figures 3 and 4, one can see that now memory accesses\nL250@P5: have become much too slow. Then any tabulated implementation is penalized, except for extremely small\nL251@P5: modulus. NTL’s implementation is also penalized, both because of better integer operations and because\nL252@P5: of a pretty bad flooring (casting to integer) of the floating point representation. Now, for Montgomery\nL253@P5: reduction, this trick is very efficient for the multiplication. However; it seems that it becomes less useful\nL254@P5: as the machine division improves as shown by the AXPY results of figure 4. As shown section 2.2 the\nL255@P5: Montgomery AXPY is less impressive because one has to compute first the multiplication, one reduction\nL256@P5: and then only the addition and tests. This is due to our choice of representation aB. “Zpz”, not suffering\nL257@P5: from this distinction between multiplied and added values can perform the multiplication and addition\nL258@P5: before the reduction so that one test and sometimes a correction by p are saved. Nevertheless, we will see\nL259@P5: in next section that our choice of representation is not anymore a disadvantage for the dot product.\nL260@P5: 3 Dot products\nL261@P5: In this section, we extend the results of [4, §3.1]. To main techniques are used: regrouping operations\nL262@P5: before performing the remaindering, and performing this remaindering only on demand. Several new\nL263@P5: variants of the representations of section 2 are tested and compared. For “GFq” and “Montgomery”\nL264@P5: representations the dot products are of course performed with their representations. In particular the\nL265@P5: timings presented do not include conversions. The argument is that the dot product is computed to be\nL266@P5: used within another higher computation. Therefore the conversion will only be useful for reading the\nL267@P5: values in the beginning and for writing the result at the end of the whole program.\nL268@P5: 3.1 53 and 64 bits\nL269@P5: The first idea is to use a representation where no overflow can occur during the course of the dot product\nL270@P5: so that the division is delayed to the end of the product: if the available mantissa is of m bits and the\nL271@P5: modulo is p, the division happens at worst every λ multiplications where λ verifies the following condition:\nL272@P5: λ(p − 1)2 < 2\nL273@P5: m (1)\nL274@P5: For instance when using 64 bits integers with numbers modulo 40009, a dot product of vectors of size lower\nL275@P5: than 1.151010 will never overflow. Therefore one has just to perform the AXPY without any division. A\nL276@P5: single machine remaindering is needed at the end for the whole computation. This produces very good\nL277@P5: speed ups for 53 (double representation) and 64 bits storage as shown in figure 5. There, the floating point\nL278@P5: representation performs the division “`a la NTL” using a floating point precomputation of the inverse, so\nL279@P5: that it is slightly better than the 64−bit integer representation. Note also the very good behavior of an\nL280@P5: implementation of P. Zimmermann [14] of Montgomery reduction over 32−bit integers.\nL281@P5: 3.2 AXPY blocks\nL282@P5: The extension of this idea is to specialize dot product in order to make several multiplications and\nL283@P5: additions before performing the division (which is then delayed), even with a small storage. Indeed, one\nL284@P5: needs to perform a division only when the intermediate result is able to overflow.\nL285@P5: Blocked dot product\nL286@P5: res = 0;\nL287@P5: unsigned long i=0; if (K<DIM) while ( i < (DIM/K)*K ) {\nL288@P5: for(unsigned long j = 0; j < K; ++j, ++i) res += a[i]*b[i];\nL289@P5: res %= P;\nL290@P5: }\nL291@P5: for(; i< DIM; ++i) res += a[i]*b[i];\nL292@P5-6: res %= P;Finite field dot product 7\nL293@P6:  0\nL294@P6:  100\nL295@P6:  200\nL296@P6:  300\nL297@P6:  400\nL298@P6:  500\nL299@P6:  600\nL300@P6:  700\nL301@P6:  800\nL302@P6:  900\nL303@P6:  0 5000 10000 15000 20000 25000 30000 35000 40000\nL304@P6: Speed (Mop/s)\nL305@P6: Prime\nL306@P6: Dot product of a vector with 512 elements on a PIII 993 MHz\nL307@P6: Classic. (1)\nL308@P6: GFq. (2)\nL309@P6: Montgomery. (3)\nL310@P6: delayed unsigned long long, 64 bits. (5)\nL311@P6: delayed double, 53 bits mantissa. (6)\nL312@P6: Fig. 5. Dot product by delayed division, on a PIII\nL313@P6: This method will be referred as “block-XXX”. Figure 6 shows that this method is optimal for small\nL314@P6: primes since it performs nearly one arithmetic operation per processor cycle. Then, the step shape of the\nL315@P6: curve reflects the thresholds when an additional division is made.\nL316@P6: Choice of Montgomery representation We see here, that our choice of representation (aB) for\nL317@P6: Montgomery is interesting. Indeed, the basic dot product operation is a cumulative AXPY. Now, each\nL318@P6: one of the added values is in fact the result of a multiplication. Therefore, the additions are between\nL319@P6: elements of the form xiB2, so that the reduction can indeed be delayed.\nL320@P6: Centered representation Another idea is to use a centered representation for “Z/pZ”: indeed if ele\u0002ments are stored between −\nL321@P6: p−1\nL322@P6: 2\nL323@P6: and p−1\nL324@P6: 2\nL325@P6: , one can double the sizes of the blocks (equation 1 now becomes\nL326@P6: λcentered(\nL327@P6: p−1\nL328@P6: 2\nL329@P6: )\nL330@P6: 2 < 2m−1\nL331@P6: ).\nL332@P6: 3.3 Division on demand\nL333@P6: The second idea is to let the overflow occur ! Then one should detect this overflow and correct the result\nL334@P6: if needed. Indeed, suppose that we have added a product ab to the accumulated result t and that an\nL335@P6: overflow has occurred. The variable t now contains actually t−2\nL336@P6: m. Well, the idea is just to precompute a\nL337@P6: correction CORR = 2m mod p and add this correction whenever an overflow has occurred. Now for the\nL338@P6: overflow detection, we use the following trick: since 0 < ab < 2\nL339@P6: m, an overflow has occurred if and only if\nL340@P6-7: t + ab < t! The “Z/pZ” code now should look like the following:8 Jean-Guillaume Dumas\nL341@P7:  0\nL342@P7:  100\nL343@P7:  200\nL344@P7:  300\nL345@P7:  400\nL346@P7:  500\nL347@P7:  600\nL348@P7:  700\nL349@P7:  800\nL350@P7:  900\nL351@P7:  0 5000 10000 15000 20000 25000 30000 35000 40000\nL352@P7: Speed (Mop/s)\nL353@P7: Prime\nL354@P7: Dot product of a vector with 512 elements on a PIII 993 MHz\nL355@P7: Classic. (1)\nL356@P7: Montgomery. (3)\nL357@P7: block−ZpZ. (1b)\nL358@P7: block−Montg. (3b)\nL359@P7: block−centered. (4b)\nL360@P7: double. (6)\nL361@P7: Fig. 6. Dot product by blocked and delayed division, on a PIII\nL362@P7: Overflow detection trick\nL363@P7: sum = 0;\nL364@P7: for(unsigned long i = 0; i<DIM; ++i) {\nL365@P7: product = a[i]*b[i];\nL366@P7: sum += product;\nL367@P7: if (sum < product) sum += CORR;\nL368@P7: }\nL369@P7: Of course one can also apply this trick to Montgomery reduction, but as shown in figure 6 the correction\nL370@P7: now implies two reductions at the end. Indeed the trick of using a representation storing aB mod p for\nL371@P7: any element a enables to perform only one reduction at the end of each block. However at the end of\nL372@P7: the dot product, one reduction has to be performed to mod out the result, and an additional one to\nL373@P7: divide the element by B. Therefore, even though Montgomery reduction is slightly faster than machine\nL374@P7: remaindering, “overflow-Montgomery” performs k + 1 reductions when “Z/pZ” performs only k machine\nL375@P7: remaindering.\nL376@P7: The centered representation can be used also in this case. This gives the better performances for small\nL377@P7: primes. However, for this representation, the unsigned trick does not apply directly anymore. The overflow\nL378@P7: and underflow need to be detected each by two tests:\nL379@P7: Signed overflow detection trick\nL380@P7: if ((sum < product) && (product - sum < 0)) sum += CORR;\nL381@P7: else if ((product < sum) && (sum - product < 0)) sum -= CORR;\nL382@P7: }\nL383@P7-8: Thus, the total of four tests is costlier and for bigger primes this overhead is too expensive.Finite field dot product 9\nL384@P8:  0\nL385@P8:  100\nL386@P8:  200\nL387@P8:  300\nL388@P8:  400\nL389@P8:  500\nL390@P8:  600\nL391@P8:  700\nL392@P8:  800\nL393@P8:  900\nL394@P8:  0 5000 10000 15000 20000 25000 30000 35000 40000\nL395@P8: Speed (Mop/s)\nL396@P8: Prime\nL397@P8: Dot product of a vector with 512 elements on a PIII 993 MHz\nL398@P8: Montgomery. (3)\nL399@P8: overflow−Montgomery. (3c)\nL400@P8: overflow−ZpZ. (1c)\nL401@P8: overflow−zpz−centered. (4c)\nL402@P8: double. (6)\nL403@P8: Fig. 7. Overflow detection, on a PIII\nL404@P8: 3.4 Hybrid\nL405@P8: Of course, one can mix both 3.2 and 3.3 approaches and delay even the overflow test when p is small. One\nL406@P8: has just to slightly change the bound on λ so that, when adding the correction, no new overflow occur:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nright.eps (https://arxiv.org/pdf/0809.0063)\nciteturn8view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 32; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view1\",\"id\":8}); Total lines: 1795\n"
